WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.095
Every marketplace plan covers the same essential health benefits; the

00:00:04.095 --> 00:00:07.780
metal level tells you how the cost is split.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.055
Actuarial value is the share of covered costs

00:00:11.055 --> 00:00:13.922
the plan pays for a standard population.

00:00:13.922 --> 00:00:17.197
Bronze plans pay about 60 percent, silver about

00:00:17.197 --> 00:00:20.473
70, gold about 80 and platinum about 90.

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:23.749
Lower metals mean lower premiums and higher deductibles.

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:27.434
Catastrophic plans, with very high deductibles, are limited to

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:31.119
people under 30 or those with a hardship exemption.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:34.804
Marcus’s daughter, turning 26 and healthy, might pick bronze;

00:00:34.804 --> 00:00:38.080
Rosa, who expects a surgery, might pick gold.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.559
Maryland Health Connection runs the state’s individual Exchange and a Small Business

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:49.038
Health Options Program, or SHOP, for employers with up to 50 employees.

00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:54.518
In SHOP the employer picks a contribution level and one or more

00:00:54.518 --> 00:00:59.997
plans, employees enroll, and the employer receives a single consolidated bill and

00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:05.476
pays it; the marketplace or its administrator distributes premiums to the carriers.

00:01:05.476 --> 00:01:08.672
Enrollment may run through an authorized producer.

00:01:08.672 --> 00:01:13.695
Employers with fewer than 25 full-time equivalent employees and modest average

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:18.717
wages may qualify for the small business health care tax credit,

00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:23.740
available only for coverage bought through SHOP, for two consecutive years.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.689
When a plan denies a claim, refuses to authorize care or rescinds

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:33.637
coverage, that is an adverse benefit determination, and the member may appeal.

00:01:33.637 --> 00:01:37.349
The first step is an internal appeal to the

00:01:37.349 --> 00:01:40.648
plan, requested within 180 days of the denial.

00:01:40.648 --> 00:01:45.596
The plan must have the appeal reviewed by someone not involved in

00:01:45.596 --> 00:01:50.132
the original decision and, for medical judgments, by an appropriate clinician.

00:01:50.132 --> 00:01:53.844
Decisions are due within 30 days for care not

00:01:53.844 --> 00:01:57.555
yet received and 60 days for care already received.

00:01:57.555 --> 00:02:03.329
Urgent cases get an expedited decision; Maryland requires emergency grievance decisions within 24 hours.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:07.040
The member may see the file and submit evidence.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.598
If the plan upholds a denial based on medical necessity or similar

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:15.776
judgment, the member may seek external review by an independent party.

00:02:15.776 --> 00:02:20.333
In Maryland, the Insurance Administration handles complaints about adverse decisions, obtains an

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:24.511
independent medical review when needed, and its determination binds the carrier.

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:27.550
The Attorney General’s Health Education and Advocacy Unit

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.208
helps consumers file grievances and gather records.

00:02:30.208 --> 00:02:34.386
External review is free to the member and must be decided

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:38.184
within set time limits, with expedited review for urgent care.

00:02:38.184 --> 00:02:42.362
A member may also skip the internal step when the carrier

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:46.540
fails to follow its own process or the case is urgent.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.422
People who buy through the individual Exchange may qualify for a premium

00:02:51.422 --> 00:02:56.304
tax credit based on household income relative to the federal poverty level.

00:02:56.304 --> 00:03:00.780
The credit is usually paid in advance directly to the insurer,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.255
lowering the monthly premium, and is reconciled on the tax return.

00:03:05.255 --> 00:03:09.323
It is not available to people offered affordable employer coverage

00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:13.392
or eligible for Medicaid; in Maryland, adults with income below

00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:17.054
138 percent of poverty generally qualify for Medicaid instead.

00:03:17.054 --> 00:03:21.122
Cost-sharing reductions lower deductibles and copayments for enrollees between 100

00:03:21.122 --> 00:03:25.190
and 250 percent of poverty, but only in silver plans.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.445
The income ranges and credit amounts are set

00:03:28.445 --> 00:03:31.700
by Congress and change, so check current figures.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.931
The federal individual mandate penalty was reduced to zero beginning in 2019,

00:03:36.931 --> 00:03:42.163
so an individual who goes without coverage owes no federal penalty, and

00:03:42.163 --> 00:03:47.394
Maryland imposes none; Maryland instead uses an Easy Enrollment checkbox on the

00:03:47.394 --> 00:03:51.317
state tax return to connect uninsured residents with coverage.

00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:53.933
Penalties remain for employers and insurers.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:58.728
An applicable large employer, one with 50 or more full-time equivalent

00:03:58.728 --> 00:04:03.524
employees, that fails to offer affordable minimum value coverage to its

00:04:03.524 --> 00:04:07.447
full-time employees can owe the employer shared responsibility payment.

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:11.807
Insurers and group plans that violate ACA rules face federal

00:04:11.807 --> 00:04:15.730
excise taxes and state fines through the Insurance Administration.
